swehouse.c: subtract the ayanamsa's rate from the sidereal cusp speeds

The sidereal entry points subtract the ayanamsa from every cusp and from every
point in ascmc, and never subtract its rate from the corresponding speed. A
sidereal call therefore returns sidereal positions beside tropical speeds, and
the two are inconsistent by the rate of precession.

    swetest -b1.1.2000 -ut12:00 -house13.4,52.5,P -speed
      house  1   51 35  6.0567   600 43 39.5410
    swetest -b1.1.2000 -ut12:00 -house13.4,52.5,P -speed -sid1
      house  1   27 43 54.4557   600 43 39.5410     <- identical

The positions differ by the ayanamsa, 23 degrees 51 minutes here, and the
speeds do not differ at all. After this patch the sidereal speed reads
600 43 39.3962, lower by 0.1448 arc seconds per day, 4.0e-5 degrees. It is
small, but it is visible in swetest's own output, and a caller that integrates
a cusp forward from a sidereal chart accumulates it.

The rate is not general precession, and it is worth being exact about that
because the sign is not constant. swe_get_ayanamsa_ex() includes the nutation
in longitude unless SEFLG_NONUT is set, which is correct here since the
position has the same term subtracted from it. The value at J2000 is mostly
precession; what nutation dominates is the variation, which swings wider than
the steady part. Stripping it leaves 3.82e-05 degrees per day, near the 50.3
arc seconds a year of precession. With it, at four epochs; the rate
column is the rate itself, Richardson-extrapolated, and the 0.1-day
difference this patch computes reproduces it to about three digits
(8.6558e-05 against 8.6572e-05 at the second epoch, for instance):

      tjd_et        rate            NONUT rate
      2451545.0     4.0212e-05      3.8245e-05
      2415020.0     8.6572e-05      3.8228e-05
      2460013.0    -1.6104e-06      3.8249e-05
      2469000.0     7.8102e-07      3.8253e-05

So on some dates the sidereal cusp speed comes out higher than the tropical
one, not lower.

The rate is a central difference of swe_get_ayanamsa_ex() over a fifth of a
day, which costs two evaluations of it; no house computation is involved and no
cusp is recomputed. A fifth rather than a whole day because the shortest
nutation terms have periods near 5.6 days, and a full day of half-step leaves
a truncation error near 3e-7 degrees per day - under half a per cent where the
rate is large, but forty per cent of it at the epochs where nutation nearly
cancels the whole rate; 0.1 brings that error down by a factor of 25. The
tropical path is untouched.

The star-based ayanamsas want a word of their own, because they inherit
whatever a star's apparent position does and that is not always smooth over a
day. True Revati, mode 28, is the sharpest of them: at tjd_et 2582405.7 the
ayanamsa drops 5.9e-4 degrees inside half a day and then resumes its slow
rise, and the central difference reads

    step h        rate, degrees per day
      0.01            -2.746e-03
      0.05            -2.626e-03
      0.10            -2.300e-03
      1.00            -6.893e-05

so the feature is real and it is the whole-day step that averages it away, not
the tenth-day step that invents it. 0.1 recovers 84 per cent of the true rate.
What that leaves in the cusp speed is 4.5e-4 degrees per day, 1.62 arc
seconds, against a cusp 1 speed of 686.67 degrees per day at that date and
latitude 52.5. It is 6.6e-7 relative, but it does reach the last digits
swetest prints, so it is stated rather than buried. Nine of 4,000 dates
sampled between 1200 and 2700 have a feature that sharp. Over the whole span
from 1000 BC to 4100 the other 46 modes stay below 7e-06 degrees per day
between the 0.1 and the 1.0 step.

On error returns the speeds are deterministic now rather than the caller's
own memory: the arrays are zeroed before the call, so a Porphyry fallback
comes back with the rate correctly applied, and the one return that computes
nothing comes back as -dayan, about -4e-5 degrees per day, in every slot.
That is a believable-looking number in a slot that was never computed, which
is why it is said here; with the Gauquelin fallback patch also applied those
slots are exact zeros instead.

Outside the range the ephemeris covers, the star-based modes cannot be
evaluated at all. Both guarded calls fail, dayan is left at zero, and the
sidereal speeds come back exactly equal to the tropical ones, which is what
the library did before this patch. Measured at tjd 2900000.0 and 3218918.5 for
mode 28: the difference against the tropical speed is 0.000e+00 to every bit.
The formula-based modes are unaffected and keep their rate across the whole
span.

Two exclusions:

  - armc keeps its tropical rate, because armc is not shifted by the ayanamsa
    in the first place. ascmc[2] is skipped for the same reason its position
    is skipped in the loop above.
  - whole sign (W) and 0 Aries (N) cusps are excluded. They are pinned to
    sidereal sign boundaries, which do not move in the sidereal frame, so
    their speed is zero and subtracting a rate from it would make it wrong.
    In the unpatched library those two report the equal house speeds instead
    of zero, which is a separate defect, corrected by the analytic cusp speed
    patch; this one is careful not to make it worse whichever of the two is
    applied.

Scope. This corrects sidereal_houses_trad(). The other two paths are taken when
SE_SIDBIT_ECL_T0 or SE_SIDBIT_SSY_PLANE is set, and the caller does not have to
ask for either: swe_set_sid_mode() forces SE_SIDBIT_ECL_T0 for SE_SIDM_J2000,
SE_SIDM_J1900, SE_SIDM_B1950 and SE_SIDM_GALALIGN_MARDYKS. Of the 47
predefined modes those four are the only ones this patch leaves alone. Their
speeds already differ from the tropical ones without it, which is worth saying
plainly: before this patch 43 of the 47 returned exactly the tropical speed and
those four did not, and after it none of the 47 does. Those two paths project
onto a different plane and reach swe_houses_armc_ex2() with an auxiliary armc
and an auxiliary obliquity of their own, so their speeds need the rates of
those auxiliary quantities as well as of the ayanamsa, which is a larger piece
of work in a construction this patch does not otherwise touch. They keep the
limitation, and it is the same limitation they have today. For whoever
extends this, one measured fact: the auxiliary route carries none of the
term on its own - at J2000 the ECL_T0 cusp speed sits 1.3e-9 degrees per
day from the tropical one - so a bare subtraction of dayan there would
remove nearly all of the inconsistency. What it would not remove is the
part belonging to the auxiliary armc and obliquity's own rates, which I
have not measured; shipping the almost-version is a choice I did not
want to make silently, so those routes are left whole.

Independent of the cusp speed patches and of the position fixes; applies in any
order with them.
---
 swehouse.c | 52 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 51 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)
diff --git a/swehouse.c b/swehouse.c
index 1c5bbd3..2840b31 100644
--- a/swehouse.c
+++ b/swehouse.c
@@ -546,13 +546,33 @@ static int sidereal_houses_trad(double tjde,
 			   char *serr)
 {
   int i, retc = OK;
-  double ay;
+  double ay, dayan = 0;
   int ito;
   int ihs = toupper(hsys);
   int ihs2 = ihs;
 // ay = swe_get_ayanamsa(tjde);
 //fprintf(stderr, "ay=%f\n", ay);
   retc = swe_get_ayanamsa_ex(tjde, iflag, &ay, NULL);
+  /* and its rate, for the speeds. Two extra evaluations of a function
+   * that is smooth over a day; no house computation is involved. */
+  if (cusp_speed != NULL || ascmc_speed != NULL) {
+    double ay1, ay2;
+    /* One error return inside swe_houses_armc_ex2() writes nothing at all:
+     * the Sunshine declination check, reachable from here with SEFLG_MOSEPH
+     * at dates where the obliquity passes 24 degrees. The rate loops at the
+     * bottom of this function would then read whatever the caller had in
+     * the speed arrays. Deterministic zeros instead; every other path
+     * overwrites them before they are read. */
+    if (cusp_speed != NULL)
+      for (i = 0; i <= ((ihs == 'G') ? 36 : 12); i++)
+	cusp_speed[i] = 0;
+    if (ascmc_speed != NULL)
+      for (i = 0; i < SE_NASCMC; i++)
+	ascmc_speed[i] = 0;
+    if (swe_get_ayanamsa_ex(tjde - 0.1, iflag, &ay1, NULL) >= 0
+     && swe_get_ayanamsa_ex(tjde + 0.1, iflag, &ay2, NULL) >= 0)
+      dayan = swe_difdeg2n(ay2, ay1) / 0.2;	/* degrees per day */
+  }
 //fprintf(stderr, "ay=%f\n", ay);
 //fprintf(stderr, "nutl=%f\n", nutl);
   if (ihs == 'G')
@@ -583,6 +603,36 @@ static int sidereal_houses_trad(double tjde,
     ascmc[i] = swe_degnorm(ascmc[i] - ay);
   }
 //if (hsys == 'P') fprintf(stderr, " => %f\n", cusp[1]);
+  /* Every longitude above has had the ayanamsa subtracted from it and no
+   * speed has had its rate subtracted, so a sidereal call returned exactly
+   * the tropical speeds beside sidereal positions. The term is the rate of
+   * the ayanamsa, about 0.145 arc seconds per day for Fagan/Bradley, which
+   * is 4.0e-5 degrees and shows in the last digits swetest prints. armc is
+   * not shifted and keeps its tropical rate. Whole sign
+   * and 0 Aries cusps are excluded: they are pinned to sidereal sign
+   * boundaries, which do not move in the sidereal frame at all. */
+  /* Not conditioned on retc. Inside the polar circle several systems fall
+   * back to Porphyry and return ERR having written every element, and the
+   * loop above has already taken the ayanamsa off those positions; skipping
+   * the speeds there would leave exactly the inconsistency this patch exists
+   * to remove, on every date at such latitudes. The one early return that
+   * writes nothing is the Sunshine declination check; the speed arrays are
+   * zeroed before the call above, so these loops read defined values on
+   * that path too. */
+  if (dayan != 0) {
+    /* The whole sign and 0 Aries carve-out is about cusps: those sit on
+     * sidereal sign boundaries and do not move in the sidereal frame at all.
+     * ascmc is not a house system quantity, the loop above shifts it by the
+     * ayanamsa for every letter including W and N, and its speeds need the
+     * rate for every letter too. */
+    if (cusp_speed != NULL && ihs != 'W' && ihs != 'N')
+      for (i = 1; i <= ito; i++)
+	cusp_speed[i] -= dayan;
+    if (ascmc_speed != NULL)
+      for (i = 0; i < SE_NASCMC; i++)
+	if (i != 2)	/* armc */
+	  ascmc_speed[i] -= dayan;
+  }
   return retc;
 }
 
